  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nCurrently in a calm period before rain chances return later this \nmorning. Totals across the area yesterday amounted to 1 and a half \nto over 2, and even isolated 3+ inch amounts up through the valley. \nMuch needed rainfall for our area.\n\nToday, we're expecting one last push of rain showers for the area \nwhen the frontal system draped to our south brings a weak low center \ncurrently in eastern Mississippi to our southeast border before \nheading out. Subtle shortwave energy will round the base of the \ntrough moving overhead today. Increased jet dynamics and thus, \nevacuation aloft, will support the development of showers mainly \nalong the I-40 corridor and south. Just above a half inch of \nadditional rainfall is forecast for the southern valley and east to \nthe border with North Carolina. But much like yesterday, activity \nthat ends up training and dumping heavier rainfall amounts, may lead \nto values above that. A marginal risk for flash flooding is still in \nplace with lesser rainfall amounts expected in SW Virginia and \nnortheastern Tennessee, however. Embedded thunder could be possible \nif a shower becomes tall enough. \n\nSome light activity could linger for far southeastern areas through \nearly tomorrow before surface high pressure dives south and east \nreplacing the unsettled weather. The rest of the week beginning \ntomorrow can be characterized as dry, temperatures just above normal \nalbeit cooler, and with northwesterly flow. A return of \nprecipitation chances possibly won't be until next weekend.",
